  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nThe persistent upper ridge over the region is beginning to break\ndown with a low pressure system moving across western Canada. With\nupper level troughing passing through the region, windy \nconditions still persist through the rest of the day. The cold \nfront earlier this morning and increased 700 mb winds maintain \ngusty west northwest winds and continue to push a cooling \nairmass southeastward. The dry airmass and gusty winds will keep \nfire weather conditions at critical levels, so the Red Flag \nWarning will continue through 9 pm tonight. Winds will also be \nstrong enough for Lake Wind Advisory for today and tomorrow. The\nwindy conditions will bring additional smoke into the region from\nWashington and Oregon, for reductions of visibility and air \nquality. Areas of dense smoke will last through the evening. A\nDense Smoke Advisory is in place until 6 PM.\n\nA second cold front will arrive tonight into Monday as a trailing\nlobe of the upper low moves over the region. This will usher in\nanother drop in temperatures for early in the week offering a \nrelief from the heat and fire weather concerns. There may be an \nisolated to scattered dry thunderstorm tonight with this, and may\nwarrant an extension of the Red Flag Warning due to receptive\nfuels. While ERC values have lowered below the 90th percentile,\nthe location of highest chances for thunder overlap with areas of\ndrier fuels.\n\nLonger term, ensembles begin to show the trough moving downstream\nas a new ridge becomes established over the Pacific Northwest. \nThis will extend height rises over eastern Montana with northwest \nflow aloft and moderating temperature toward seasonable averages \nlate week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nWhile NBM handled the big picture well across the forecast, a\nblend with NBM90 was used for near term winds this weekend in line\nwith the expected windy conditions supporting the Red Flag and\nLake Wind headlines in effect.",
